As a young man in Antioch, John was so afraid of becoming a bishop that he hid and let his dearest friend be seized instead. Years later imperial officers spirited him out of the city to make him archbishop of Constantinople. There he preached over a fallen consul shaking under the altar, called the poor man in the street an altar of Christ, and defied an empress. Soldiers broke up his Easter baptisms, a mob of monks drove him from his sickbed, and guards marched him until he collapsed. He died a prisoner, dressed in white, saying the words he always used: "Glory to God for all things." How does a man who loses everything die giving thanks?
